        -:   92:/// \class RTree
        -:   93:/// Implementation of RTree, a multidimensional bounding rectangle tree.
        -:   94:/// Example usage: For a 3-dimensional tree use RTree<Object*, float, 3> myTree;
        -:   95:///
        -:   96:/// This modified, templated C++ version by Greg Douglas at Auran (http://www.auran.com)
        -:   97:///
        -:   98:/// DATATYPE Referenced data, should be int, void*, obj* etc. no larger than sizeof<void*> and simple type
        -:   99:/// ELEMTYPE Type of element such as int or float
        -:  100:/// NUMDIMS Number of dimensions such as 2 or 3
        -:  101:/// ELEMTYPEREAL Type of element that allows fractional and large values such as float or double, for use in volume calcs
        -:  102:///
        -:  103:/// NOTES: Inserting and removing data requires the knowledge of its constant Minimal Bounding Rectangle.
        -:  104:///        This version uses new/delete for nodes, I recommend using a fixed size allocator for efficiency.
        -:  105:///        Instead of using a callback function for returned results, I recommend and efficient pre-sized, grow-only memory
        -:  106:///        array similar to MFC CArray or STL Vector for returning search query result.
        -:  107:///
        -:  108:template <class DATATYPE, class ELEMTYPE, int NUMDIMS,
        -:  109:          class ELEMTYPEREAL = ELEMTYPE, int TMAXNODES = 8, int TMINNODES = TMAXNODES / 2>
